{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head c665af412e500bde23d9f51d250353a96de5e3de, citing all fifteen per-path artifacts as the implementation of f209cd59. One unpinned claim is recorded below; it is a note, not a blocker, and I verified the claim myself rather than leaving it as an open question.\n\nI REPRODUCED THE FAILURE BEFORE JUDGING THE FIX, in both directions, because a hardening whose failure I have not seen is one I am taking on trust. With GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null and a GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL carrying commit.gpgsign=true, core.hooksPath and init.defaultBranch: at parent 9a1e6b1c1, cmd/gitseq-mcp TestReviewTool fails with \"fatal: failed to write commit object\". At this head, the same command is ok in 9.666s. cmd/gs TestSuccessionAdmissionPreflightsEveryActWithoutAppending, the other test my request named, is ok in 1.616s under the same hostile config. Adding command-scoped injection on top - GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 with KEY_0=commit.gpgsign and VALUE_0=true - internal/kernel is ok in 180.767s.\n\nCOVERAGE IS COMPLETE, AND I CHECKED IT BY ENUMERATION RATHER THAN BY COUNTING ARTIFACTS. My request said a fix reaching only the two sites I happened to probe would be worse than none, because it reads as coverage. So I listed every test package that invokes git and every package carrying the testgit hook, and compared the sets. They are identical but for one entry: internal/testgit itself, which must run without the hook in order to test it. That is correct by design rather than a gap. Fifteen packages, fifteen artifacts, one deliberate exception.\n\nTHE MECHANISM IS THE RIGHT SHAPE AND ITS OWN COMMENT ARGUES THE CASE HONESTLY. Isolate sets GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM, points GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL at os.DevNull, and unsets GIT_CONFIG_COUNT, GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS and every GIT_CONFIG_KEY_ and GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_ variable. The package comment states why the boundary is process-wide rather than a list of -c overrides at each call site: Git is invoked both by fixtures and by the code they exercise, so a per-call-site fix would be an incomplete list maintained forever. That is the cost argument my request asked for, reached independently and stated in the code rather than only in a report. Splitting Main from Run so packages with existing TestMain cleanup keep it is the right seam; cmd/gs and internal/docset use it exactly that way.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME AT THIS HEAD. Disabling the command-scoped clearing - renaming the matched variables so the loop never fires - fails TestIsolateClearsCommandScopedConfigInjection while TestIsolateIgnoresGlobalAndSystemGitConfig still passes. That is targeted at the decision site rather than a blanket break, which is what makes it evidence. Tree byte-clean after reverting.\n\nTHE ONE FINDING, AND IT IS THE RISK I SET OUT TO LOOK FOR. A process-level scrub can be too wide as easily as too narrow. Too narrow leaves packages exposed; too wide masks a repository's own deliberate configuration and hides real defects. The design's answer is that repository-local configuration remains available - and that claim is asserted three times, in the package comment, in Isolate's own doc comment, and in the review conditions - while nothing tests it. There are exactly two tests, covering global/system isolation and command-scoped clearing. Neither touches repo-local visibility.\n\nSo I verified it myself rather than reporting an open question: after calling Isolate, I initialised a repository, set planner.probe with git config --local, and read it back. It is visible. The claim is true, and it is structurally sound for a reason worth writing down - Isolate touches only system, global and command-scoped state, and never anything repository-scoped.\n\nI am not blocking, because the claim holds and the mechanism cannot plausibly break it today. But it is the load-bearing half of the design and it is unpinned. If someone later reaches for GIT_CONFIG_NOGLOBAL semantics, or wraps git with a -c layer, repo-local visibility could go silently and no assertion would fire. It cost me thirty lines to prove; a third test in that file would keep it proved. Worth doing when the file is next touched, and recording here so it is a deliberate omission rather than an unnoticed one.\n\nARCHITECTURE. This is test infrastructure and it changes no production contract. internal/testgit is a new test-only package; the fourteen other artifacts are one nine-line TestMain hook each, except cmd/gs and internal/docset which route existing TestMain functions through Run. No layer from docs/reference/architecture.md changes its contract, and the page needs no update. The one architectural judgement worth naming is that the boundary lives in the test process rather than in production code, so nothing shipped to a user is affected by it.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary here is between a contributor's machine and the test suite, and the change strengthens it. Ambient system and global configuration and command-scoped injection are excluded wholesale, so a contributor's signing key, hooks path, line-ending policy, commit template or default branch cannot silently change what the suite proves. Hooks in particular matter: core.hooksPath from a global config could otherwise run arbitrary code during a test commit, and that is now excluded. No secret is read or written, no bound moves, and the failure mode is explicit - Isolate returns an error and Run exits 2 with a message rather than proceeding on a half-applied boundary. Repository-local configuration deliberately remains visible, which is a correct narrowing rather than a hole, since it is under the repository's own control and some tests exercise it on purpose.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The TestMain surface is already minimal: nine lines per package, no options, no configuration. My request invited a judgement on per-site configuration versus a shared helper versus a process-level scrub, and the head chose the third with the reason recorded in the package comment. I looked for a way to reduce the fifteen hooks to fewer and there is not one that Go's test model allows - TestMain is per-package by construction. The only reduction available would be to drop packages from coverage, which is the defect this repairs.\n\nGATES, run by me from a clean detached checkout at the exact head, worktree byte-clean after mutation: gofmt silent, git diff --check clean, go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 34 packages ok.\n\nCodex may merge this exact head once this verdict is ratified. Any change to the head returns it to review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:831f7d0095fdd68b944d29b8e424d3c4a3ca6dd9","head":"c665af412e500bde23d9f51d250353a96de5e3de","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c1385f9b407cf341295b2c20747fab1c86c78aa7\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e38d89564bfb93b862cf96ab128cc937251838a6","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}